use std
into draco.amni

Namespace draco; imports math and binary. Decodes Draco compressed triangle meshes (bitstream version 2.2, the one every current encoder writes, including the glTF extension KHR_draco_mesh_compression), written to match the Draco reference decoder value for value: the same points in the same order, the same faces and the same attribute values. Nothing here may crash, loop forever or read past the end of a damaged stream: every read is checked, every index is checked before it is used, and a stream that is wrong in any way makes `decode` give false with a short reason in `why`.

Whole numbers here are 64-bit `int`. Where the reference uses 32-bit integers the value is kept to 32 bits at the points named below with `w32`, which gives the low 32 bits of a whole number as a signed two's complement number; `u32of` gives the low 32 bits as an unsigned number. `idiv` is C integer division (the quotient truncated toward zero; division by zero never happens because callers check). Float work named "in single precision" is done in `f32` with every operation rounded to single precision like C `float`, never fused into a multiply-add.

Output (public module state, filled by `decode`): `faces`, a list of `u32`, three point numbers per triangle; `points`, the number of points; and one entry per decoded attribute in the order the stream declares them, in the int lists `att_type` (0 position, 1 normal, 2 color, 3 texture coordinate, 4 generic, 5 to 8 other named kinds), `att_dtype` (the Draco data type: 1 int8, 2 uint8, 3 int16, 4 uint16, 5 int32, 6 uint32, 7 int64, 8 uint64, 9 float32, 10 float64, 11 bool), `att_comps` (components per value), `att_norm` (1 when the stream marks the attribute normalized, else 0), `att_uid` (the attribute's unique id) and `att_first` (where its values start in `values`); `values` is a list of `real` holding, for each attribute, `points` times its component count values, point by point. `why` is a growable byte list holding the reason for the last failure, and `error()` gives it as a read-only byte slice. `find(uid)` gives the attribute number whose unique id is uid, or -1. `fail(msg)` replaces `why` with msg and gives false; every failure below goes through `fail` with a short reason naming the step.

Reader state: `buf`, a growable byte list holding a copy of the stream, `pos`, the read position, and `bad`, a bool. `u8v()` gives the byte at `pos` and moves on, or sets `bad` and gives 0 when `pos` is at or past the end of `buf`; `i8v()` is the same read as a signed byte. `u16v()`, `i32v()`, `u32v()` read 2 or 4 bytes little-endian (unsigned, signed, unsigned) and `f32v()` reads 4 bytes as the IEEE single-precision float with those bits (an `f32`, through `binary::f32_from_bits`); each sets `bad` and gives 0 without moving when fewer bytes are left. `varintv(limit)` reads an unsigned LEB128 number: each byte adds its low 7 bits, the first byte lowest, and a byte below 128 ends it; it sets `bad` and gives 0 when the bytes run out or when more than limit bytes would be needed (limit is 5 for 32-bit numbers and 10 for 64-bit sizes), and a 32-bit read whose value does not fit in 32 bits also sets `bad`. `left()` is the number of bytes from `pos` to the end of `buf`.

Bit reader state (the reference's bit mode on a byte buffer): `bit_at` (the first byte), `bit_end` (one past the last byte it may read) and `bit_off` (bits used so far). `bits_begin(at, end)` sets them and zeroes `bit_off`. `bits(n)` gives an n-bit unsigned number built from single bits, lowest bit first: bit number `bit_off` is bit (`bit_off` mod 8) of byte `bit_at` plus `bit_off` integer-divided by 8, and only a bit that lies before `bit_end` is used and moves `bit_off` on; a bit past the end counts as 0 and does not move. n above 32 sets `bad` and gives 0. `bits_bytes()` is `bit_off` plus 7, integer-divided by 8.

Bit decoders (the reference's RAnsBitDecoder; several are in use at once, so they live in parallel int lists `rb_data` (the first byte of the decoder's data in `buf`), `rb_off`, `rb_state` and `rb_p0`, emptied by `decode`). `rbit_start()` reads the zero probability with `u8v()` and a 32-bit `varintv` size; it fails (gives -1) when `bad` is set, the size is 0 or larger than `left()`. Let b be the last byte of the data (at `pos` plus size minus 1) and x its top two bits: x 0 gives state b bitwise-and 63 with offset size minus 1; x 1 needs size at least 2 and gives the 16-bit little-endian number ending at that byte bitwise-and 0x3FFF with offset size minus 2; x 2 needs size at least 3 and gives the 24-bit little-endian number ending there bitwise-and 0x3FFFFF with offset size minus 3; x 3 fails. Then state goes up by 4096 and the start fails when state is at least 4096 times 256. On success the decoder's data starts at `pos`, `pos` moves on by size, the four values are pushed and its number is given. `rbit(k)` reads the next bit of decoder k: p is 256 minus its zero probability; when its state is below 4096 and its offset is above 0, the offset goes down by one and the state becomes state times 256 plus the data byte at that offset; then with q the state integer-divided by 256 and r the state mod 256, the bit is r below p, and the state becomes q times p plus r when the bit is set and state minus q times p minus p otherwise. It gives the bit as a bool.

Symbol decoder (the reference's RAnsSymbolDecoder; one at a time, module state `rs_prob`, `rs_cum` and `rs_lut` int lists, `rs_precision`, `rs_data`, `rs_off`, `rs_state`). `rans_table(bits)` reads a probability table: the precision exponent P is 3 times bits integer-divided by 2, at least 12 and at most 20, and `rs_precision` is 2 to the P. The symbol count n is a 32-bit `varintv`; it fails when n integer-divided by 64 is above `left()`. For each symbol i from 0: read byte d with `u8v()`; when d bitwise-and 3 is 3 then o is d shifted right by 2, it fails when i plus o is at least n, and symbols i to i plus o all get probability 0 and i moves past them; otherwise the probability is d shifted right by 2, then for each of the d bitwise-and 3 extra bytes e (number b from 0) it is bitwise-ored with e shifted left by 8 times (b plus 1) minus 2. Then the cumulative table: each symbol's `rs_cum` is the sum of the probabilities before it, the running sum must never pass `rs_precision`, `rs_lut` maps every slot from its cumulative value up to (not including) the next to the symbol, and the final sum must equal `rs_precision` exactly, or it fails. A table with zero symbols is allowed here. It gives the symbol count, or -1 on failure. `rans_begin()` reads a 64-bit `varintv` byte count c, fails when c is above `left()` or below 1, sets the data to start at `pos`, moves `pos` on by c and starts the state from the last data byte the same way `rbit_start` does except that x 3 needs c at least 4 and gives the 32-bit little-endian number ending at that byte bitwise-and 0x3FFFFFFF with offset c minus 4; the state then goes up by 4 times `rs_precision` and it fails when the state is at least 4 times `rs_precision` times 256. `rans_next()` gives one symbol: while the state is below 4 times `rs_precision` and the offset is above 0, the offset goes down by one and the state becomes state times 256 plus that data byte; then with q the state integer-divided by `rs_precision` and r the state mod `rs_precision`, the symbol s is `rs_lut` at r and the state becomes q times the probability of s plus r minus the cumulative of s.

`symbols(n, comps, out)` decodes n unsigned values into the int list out (appending) and gives true when it worked. n 0 gives true at once. Otherwise it reads a scheme byte. Scheme 0 (tagged): `rans_table(5)` then `rans_begin()`, failing when either fails or the table has no symbols; then `bits_begin(pos, length of buf)`; for each group of comps values (while fewer than n values are out) the bit length is `rans_next()` and each of the comps values is `bits(length)` (stop early once n values are out); afterwards `pos` moves on by `bits_bytes()`. Scheme 1 (raw): a byte m that must be from 1 to 18, `rans_table(m)` failing on no symbols, `rans_begin()`, then n values each `rans_next()`. Any other scheme, `bad`, or a failing step fails.

Metadata (header flag 0x8000) is skipped, not kept: `skip_meta()` reads a 32-bit `varintv` count of attribute entries; each is a 32-bit `varintv` id followed by one metadata tree; then one more tree for the whole mesh. `skip_tree()` keeps a count of pending blocks starting at 1 (the root, which has no name); while blocks are pending it takes one, reads its name when it is not the root (a length byte and that many bytes), reads a 32-bit `varintv` entry count and for each entry a name, a 32-bit `varintv` size that must be from 1 to `left()`, and that many bytes; then a 32-bit `varintv` sub-block count that must not be above `left()`, added to the pending count. Any `bad` read fails.

Corner tables. Corners are numbered 3 times the face plus 0, 1 or 2; -1 means no corner or no vertex. `nxt(c)` is -1 for c below 0, c minus 2 when c mod 3 is 2 and c plus 1 otherwise; `prv(c)` is -1 for c below 0, c plus 2 when c mod 3 is 0 and c minus 1 otherwise. The main table is `c2v` (corner to vertex), `opp` (corner to opposite corner) and `lmc` (vertex to its left-most corner; its length is the main vertex count). Attribute tables, one per attribute-data slot k from 0 to `n_ad` minus 1, live in flat lists: `a_c2v` and `a_seam` (corner flags) hold `nc` (the corner count) entries per slot at k times `nc`; `a_vseam` holds the main vertex count of flags per slot at k times that count; `a_lmc` holds each slot's left-most corners starting at `a_lmc_at[k]` with `a_nv[k]` of them. A table number t is -1 for the main table and k for slot k. `vert(t, c)` is -1 for c below 0 or not below `nc`, else `c2v[c]` or slot t's `a_c2v` entry. `oppo(t, c)` is -1 for c out of range, else `opp[c]` for the main table and -1 for a slot when its `a_seam` flag of c is set, otherwise `opp[c]`. `lm(t, v)` is -1 when v is below 0 or not below that table's vertex count, else the left-most corner. `nverts(t)` is the main vertex count or `a_nv[t]`. `swl(t, c)` is `nxt(oppo(t, nxt(c)))` and `swr(t, c)` is `prv(oppo(t, prv(c)))`. `boundary(t, v)` is true when `swl(t, lm(t, v))` is -1. `on_seam(k, c)` is slot k's `a_vseam` flag of the main vertex `vert(-1, c)` (false when that is -1). `set_opp(a, b)` sets `opp[a]` to b and `opp[b]` to a.

`around(t, c, out)` clears the int list out and appends the corners around the vertex of corner c in the reference's VertexCornersIterator order: c first; then repeatedly `swl` from the last corner while it is not -1 and not c; when that walk ends at -1 (not back at c), continue from c with `swr` repeatedly until -1. Every corner appended is valid. A guard of 3 times `nc` plus 3 steps stops a damaged table from looping.

Edgebreaker connectivity, `edgebreaker()`, after the header and the traversal type byte (0 standard, 2 valence, saved in `valence`). Read with 32-bit `varintv`s: the encoded vertex count V and face count F; fail when V is above 3 times F, or when V times (V minus 1) integer-divided by 2 is below 3 times F integer-divided by 2. Then the attribute-data count `n_ad` (a byte), the symbol count S and the split-symbol count Q, both `varintv`; fail when S is above F, F is above S plus S integer-divided by 3, or Q is above S. Also fail when F is above 256 times the stream length plus 1024. `nc` is 3 times F; `c2v` and `opp` get `nc` entries of -1, `lmc` starts empty and may grow to at most V plus Q vertices (`maxv`), and `hole` gets `maxv` entries true. Topology splits: a 32-bit `varintv` count that must not be above F; then for each a source delta and a split delta (`varintv`s): source is the delta plus the previous source (starting 0), the split delta must not be above source, split is source minus the split delta; these go into the lists `sp_src` and `sp_split`. Then with `bits_begin(pos, length of buf)` one bit per split gives `sp_edge`, and `pos` moves on by `bits_bytes()`.

Traversal start. Standard: a 64-bit `varintv` size z that must not be above `left()`; the symbol bits start at `pos` (`bits_begin(pos, length of buf)`, so a damaged count reads on into later bytes like the reference) and `pos` moves on by z. Then (both kinds) the start-face decoder `rbit_start()`, then one seam decoder per attribute-data slot with `rbit_start()`, all failing on -1. Valence only: `valences` gets `maxv` zeros; for each of 6 contexts a 32-bit `varintv` count that must not be above F, then `symbols(count, 1, ...)` into that context's list (flat list `ctx_sym` with `ctx_at` and `ctx_left` per context, `ctx_left` starting at the count); `active` starts at -1. The position after all this is remembered as the attribute data start.

`next_symbol()`: standard reads `bits(1)`, and when it is 1 reads `bits(2)` and gives 1 bitwise-or that shifted left by 1 (so C 0, S 1, L 3, R 5, E 7). Valence: when `active` is -1 it gives E (7); otherwise `ctx_left[active]` goes down by one, and when it is below 0 or the symbol there is above 4 it gives 99 (invalid); the symbol 0, 1, 2, 3, 4 maps to C, S, L, R, E. `reached(c)` (valence only, after every symbol with the corner on top of the stack) with n `nxt(c)` and p `prv(c)` adds to `valences` of `vert(-1, ·)`: for the last symbol C or S, n and p get 1 each; for R, c gets 1, n gets 1, p gets 2; for L, c gets 1, n gets 2, p gets 1; for E, all three get 2; then `active` is the valence of n clamped to 2..7, minus 2. Merging vertex b into a during S adds b's valence to a's.

The symbol loop runs S times with symbol id i and new face f (counting from 0, corner g is 3 times f), keeping a corner stack and a map from decoder symbol id to a corner (`sp_corner`, -1 when unset). Every corner taken from the stack or computed must be valid (0 or more) before use or it fails. C: a is the top; x is `vert(-1, nxt(a))`; b is `nxt(lm(-1, x))`; fail when a equals b or either has an opposite; `set_opp(a, g plus 1)`, `set_opp(b, g plus 2)`; ap is `vert(-1, prv(a))`, bn is `vert(-1, nxt(b))`, fail when x equals ap or bn; corners g, g plus 1, g plus 2 get vertices x, bn, ap; `lmc[ap]` is g plus 2; `hole[x]` false; the top becomes g. R or L: a is the top and must have no opposite; for R the opposite corner o is g plus 2, l is g plus 1, r is g; for L o is g plus 1, l is g, r is g plus 2; `set_opp(o, a)`; a new vertex (appended to `lmc`, failing past `maxv`) goes to corner o with left-most o; vr is `vert(-1, prv(a))`, corner r gets vr and `lmc[vr]` becomes r; corner l gets `vert(-1, nxt(a))`; the top becomes g; then the split check. S: b is the top and is popped; when `sp_corner` of i is set it is pushed; the stack must not be empty; a is the top; fail when a equals b or either has an opposite; `set_opp(a, g plus 2)`, `set_opp(b, g plus 1)`; vp is `vert(-1, prv(a))`; corner g gets vp, g plus 1 gets `vert(-1, nxt(a))`; bp is `vert(-1, prv(b))`, corner g plus 2 gets bp and `lmc[bp]` is g plus 2; n is `nxt(b)` and vn its vertex; merge valences of vn into vp; `lmc[vp]` becomes `lmc[vn]`; then starting from n, while the corner is not -1: it gets vertex vp and moves to `swl(-1, ·)`, failing when it comes back to n; `lmc[vn]` becomes -1 and, when `n_ad` is 0, vn is added to the list of removed vertices; the top becomes g. E: three new vertices for corners g, g plus 1, g plus 2 (failing past `maxv`), each with its own corner as left-most; g is pushed; then the split check. Any other symbol fails. After the symbol, for valence, `reached(top)`. The split check: e is S minus i minus 1; while the last split exists: when its source is above e it fails; when it equals e it is popped, and with the stack top t the new corner is `nxt(t)` when its edge bit is 1 and `prv(t)` otherwise, stored in `sp_corner` at S minus its split minus 1; otherwise stop.

After the loop fail when the vertex count is above `maxv`. Then while the stack is not empty, pop corner c and read the start-face bit: when set (an interior start), fail when the face count is already F; a is c, vn is `vert(-1, nxt(a))`, b is `nxt(lm(-1, vn))`, vx is `vert(-1, nxt(b))`, cc is `nxt(lm(-1, vx))`; the three corners must be valid, different and have no opposites; vp is `vert(-1, nxt(cc))`; a new face with corner h: `set_opp(h, a)`, `set_opp(h plus 1, b)`, `set_opp(h plus 2, cc)`, vertices vx, vp, vn, and `hole` false for all three. At the end the face count must be F. Removed vertices are then compacted: m starts at the vertex count; for each removed vertex r in order: s is m minus 1, and while `lmc[s]` is -1, m goes down by one and s is m minus 1 (fail when m reaches 0); when s is below r skip it; otherwise every corner in `around(-1, lmc[s])` must have vertex s and gets vertex r, `lmc[r]` becomes `lmc[s]`, `lmc[s]` becomes -1, `hole[r]` takes `hole[s]` and `hole[s]` becomes false, and m goes down by one. The connectivity vertex count is m (the vertex list keeps its full length).

Seams, when `n_ad` is above 0: for every face f in order, for its corners c, `nxt(c)`, `prv(c)` (c is 3 times f): when the corner has no opposite it is a seam for every slot; otherwise when the opposite's face is at least f, read one bit per slot from that slot's seam decoder, a set bit making it a seam for that slot. Marking corner c a seam for slot k sets its `a_seam` flag and the `a_vseam` flags of the main vertices of `nxt(c)` and `prv(c)`, and when c has an opposite o, sets o's `a_seam` flag and the flags of the vertices of `nxt(o)` and `prv(o)`. Then each slot's table is rebuilt (the reference's RecomputeVertices): its `a_c2v` entries start at -1 and its vertex list empty; for each main vertex v with `lmc[v]` not -1: a new slot vertex number; first is `lmc[v]`; when v's `a_vseam` flag is set, walk `swl(k, ·)` from first while not -1, failing when it returns to `lmc[v]`, keeping the last valid corner as first; first gets the new vertex and is its left-most; then from `swr(-1, first)` while the corner is not -1 and not first: when the `a_seam` flag of `nxt` of that corner is set, a further new vertex starts with this corner as its left-most; the corner gets the current vertex; move on with `swr(-1, ·)`.

Points (the reference's AssignPointsToCorners). When `n_ad` is 0 the faces are the corner vertices and `points` is the connectivity vertex count. Otherwise `cp` (corner to point) has `nc` entries and points are counted from 0: for each main vertex v with `lmc[v]` c not -1: d is c; when `hole[v]` is false, for each slot k in order with `on_seam(k, c)`: walk a from `swr(-1, c)` while a is not c, failing on -1, and when slot k's vertex of a differs from its vertex of c, d becomes a and stop both loops. Then a new point goes to corner d; walking a from `swr(-1, d)` while a is not -1 and not d, with pc the previous corner (starting d): when any slot's vertex of a differs from its vertex of pc a new point goes to a, else a takes pc's point. `points` is the number of points made and `faces` are `cp`.

Sequential connectivity, `sequential()`: 32-bit `varintv`s F and P (the point count); fail when F is above `left()` integer-divided by 3 or P is above 256 times the stream length plus 1024. A method byte: 0 reads `symbols(3 times F, 1)`; walking the values with last starting 0, each value u gives d as u shifted right by 1, negated (failing when it is above last) when u is odd; the index is last plus d, and last becomes it. Method 1 reads every index as a byte when P is below 256, as `u16v()` when below 65536, as a 32-bit `varintv` when below 2 to the 21, and as `u32v()` otherwise. Every index must be below P. `faces` gets them and `points` is P.

Attributes. `attributes(edge)` reads the attribute-decoder count (a byte). For an edgebreaker mesh each decoder first reads its slot (`i8v()`), a type byte and a traversal byte: a slot of 0 or more must be below `n_ad` and not already taken, a negative slot means the position data and may appear once; type 0 (vertex) allows traversal 0 (depth first) or 1 (prediction degree), type 1 (corner) needs traversal 0 and a slot of 0 or more; anything else fails. A sequential mesh reads nothing here. Then for each decoder: a 32-bit `varintv` attribute count that must be at least 1 and at most 5 times `left()`; for each attribute its kind byte (below 9), data type byte (1 to 11), component byte (at least 1), normalized byte and unique id (32-bit `varintv`), appended to the output lists; then for each of the decoder's attributes in order a decoder kind byte: 0 generic, 1 integer, 2 quantized (needs float32), 3 normal (needs float32 and 3 components). After all decoders are described, each decoder decodes its attributes as below, in order.

A decoder's sequence. For a sequential mesh the entries are the points 0 to `points` minus 1 and every attribute maps each point to itself. For an edgebreaker mesh the table t is the slot for a corner-type decoder and -1 otherwise; `v2d` gets the larger of `nverts(t)` and the main vertex count entries of -1, and `d2c` and `seq` start empty. Visiting vertex v from corner c appends the point of corner c (`faces` at c) to `seq`, appends c to `d2c` and sets `v2d[v]` to the number of entries before it. The faces are walked in order from corner 0, 3, 6 and so on with the traversal (fresh visited flags for faces and vertices per decoder). Depth first from corner c: nothing when face c over 3 is visited; otherwise the stack is [c]; visit the vertices of `nxt(c)` and `prv(c)` when unvisited (marking them), failing when either is -1; then while the stack is not empty: take the top c; pop and continue when c is -1 or its face is visited; else loop: mark the face; v is `vert(t, c)` (fail on -1); when v is unvisited, note `boundary(t, v)`, mark and visit v from c, and when not on the boundary c becomes `oppo(t, nxt(c))` and the loop goes on; then r is `oppo(t, nxt(c))` and l is `oppo(t, prv(c))`, a missing corner counting as a visited face; both visited pops and leaves the loop; only r visited makes c l; only l visited makes c r; neither replaces the top with l, pushes r and leaves the loop. Prediction degree from corner c (only when there are vertices): three stacks and a best priority 0, a degree count per vertex; push c on stack 0; visit `nxt(c)`, `prv(c)` and c's vertices when unvisited; then repeatedly pop from the lowest non-empty stack at or after the best (that stack becomes the best) until all are empty; skip a visited face; else loop: mark the face, visit c's vertex when unvisited; r and l as above with visited flags; when l's face is unvisited its priority is 0 when l's vertex is visited and otherwise 1 when its degree (raised by one first) is above 1, else 2; when r's face is visited and that priority is at most the best, c becomes l and the loop goes on, else l is pushed on its priority's stack (lowering the best to it when lower); then when r's face is unvisited the same with r except that it continues whenever its priority is at most the best; then leave the loop. After the walk each attribute of the decoder maps the point of every face corner c to `v2d[vert(t, c)]`, failing when the vertex is -1, the entry is below 0, not below `points` or not below the entry count, or the point is not below `points`; a point that no face corner maps fails.

Decoding an attribute's portable values (all attributes of the decoder in order, before any transform data). Generic: for each entry read the attribute's components in its data type (little-endian, 1, 2, 4 or 8 bytes) and keep them as reals. The others read a prediction method (`i8v()`, from -2 to 6) and when it is not -2 a transform (`i8v()`, from -1 to 3). The prediction exists only for a normal attribute with transform 3, or another attribute with transform 1 (wrap); otherwise there is none. Its kind: a sequential mesh always uses delta; on an edgebreaker mesh method 1 is parallelogram, 4 constrained multi-parallelogram, 5 texture coordinates, 6 geometric normal (only for normal attributes; an integer attribute asking for it fails), 2 and 3 fail as unsupported, and anything else is delta; for a normal attribute only 6 gives the geometric normal and every other method gives delta. Methods 5 and 6 need the first position attribute already decoded with portable values and 3 components, or fail. The portable component count pc is 2 for normals and the attribute's count otherwise; n is the entry count times pc. A flag byte: when it is not 0 the values come from `symbols(n, pc)`; when 0 a width byte w: w 4 reads n `i32v()` values, any other w reads n little-endian w-byte unsigned values (failing when w is above 4 or fewer than w times n bytes are left). Unless a prediction exists and it uses the normal transform, every value u is turned from zigzag: u shifted right by 1, and minus that minus 1 when u is odd. Then the prediction data and the original values below; the result is kept as the attribute's portable values (kept to 32 bits with `w32`).

Transforms (each prediction has one; all values are 32-bit). Wrap: data is min and max (`i32v()` each), fail when min is above max or max minus min is at least 2147483647; dif is max minus min plus 1. A value from prediction p and correction e: p is clamped to min..max, v is p plus e (as 64-bit), then v minus dif when v is above max, v plus dif when v is below min. Normal: data is a value m (`i32v()`) and an ignored `i32v()`; m must be above 0 and odd; q is the index of m's highest set bit plus 1 and must be from 2 to 30; then `mq` is 2 to the q minus 1, `mv` is `mq` minus 1 and `ce` is `mv` integer-divided by 2. A value from prediction (s, t) and correction (e, f): s and t go down by `ce`; it is in the diamond when the absolute values add to at most `ce`; when not, `invert` it; it is bottom-left when both are 0 or s is below 0 and t at most 0; the rotation count r is, for s 0: 0 when t is 0, 3 when t is above 0, else 1; for s above 0: 2 when t is at least 0, else 1; for s below 0: 0 when t is at most 0, else 3. When not bottom-left rotate by r (1 gives (t, minus s), 2 gives (minus s, minus t), 3 gives (minus t, s)). The result is (`modmax(s plus e)`, `modmax(t plus f)`) where `modmax(x)` is x minus `mq` when x is above `ce`, x plus `mq` when x is below minus `ce`, else x; when not bottom-left rotate it by (4 minus r) mod 4; when the prediction was not in the diamond `invert` the result; then both go up by `ce`. `invert(s, t)`: the signs are (1, 1) when both are at least 0, (-1, -1) when both are at most 0, else each one's own sign (1 above 0, else -1); with corners cs and ct the signs times `ce`, us is 2 times s minus cs and ut is 2 times t minus ct; when the product of the signs is at least 0 the pair becomes (minus ut, minus us), otherwise it swaps; then us goes up by cs and ut by ct, each is kept with `w32`, and each is divided by 2 with `idiv`.

Predictions, given the corrections, the entry count, pc, `seq`, `d2c`, `v2d` and the table t. `posof(e)` is the position of entry e: the first position attribute's portable values at its point map entry for the point `seq[e]`, as three 64-bit numbers. Let `data(k)` read the decoded value k, which is only allowed for k already decoded; a vertex-to-entry lookup outside `v2d` or giving -1, or a corner outside the table, fails. Delta: entry 0 is the transform of a zero prediction, and each later entry uses the entry before it as its prediction. The data of delta, parallelogram and geometric normal is just the transform data; constrained multi-parallelogram and texture coordinates read their own data first (below) and then the transform data; geometric normal reads the transform data first and then a flip bit decoder with `rbit_start()`.

`para(p, c, out)` is the parallelogram prediction for entry p at corner c: o is `oppo(t, c)`; with o valid, the entries of the vertices of o, `nxt(o)` and `prv(o)` (in that order: opposite, next, previous) must all be 0 or more and below p, and then each component is next plus previous minus opposite, kept with `w32`; otherwise it gives false. Parallelogram: entry 0 from a zero prediction; each later entry p (up to the length of `d2c`) uses `para(p, d2c[p])` when it works, else the entry before it. Constrained multi-parallelogram data: for each of 4 contexts a 32-bit `varintv` flag count (at most `nc`), and when above 0 a bit decoder from which that many crease flags are read in order. Its values: entry 0 from a zero prediction; for p from 1: start at `d2c[p]` and collect up to 4 `para` predictions walking `swl(t, ·)` from the start and, once that gives -1, `swr(t, ·)` from the start (stopping when the walk returns to the start, when 4 are found, or when the right walk gives -1); with k found, for each of them take the next unused crease flag of context k minus 1 (fail when none are left) and sum the components of the ones that are not creases (adding with `w32`); when none are used the prediction is the entry before, else each sum `idiv` the used count.

Texture coordinates data: a count (`i32v()`, from 0 to the value count) and a bit decoder; with last starting true, each of count bits flips last when the bit is 0, and the orientation list gets last. Values (pc must be 2), for each p from 0 with c `d2c[p]`: nd and pd are the entries of the vertices of `nxt(c)` and `prv(c)`. When nd and pd are both below p: nu and pu are their decoded values; when equal the prediction is pu; otherwise with tip, next and prev being `posof` of p, nd and pd, pn is prev minus next and pn2 its squared length; when pn2 is not 0: cn is tip minus next, dot is pn dot cn, puv is pu minus nu; fail when the larger absolute component of nu is above 9223372036854775807 integer-divided by pn2, when the absolute dot is above that maximum integer-divided by the larger absolute component of puv, or when the absolute dot is above the maximum integer-divided by the larger absolute component of pn; xuv is nu times pn2 plus puv times dot (64-bit wraparound); xpos is next plus (dot times pn) `idiv` pn2 per component; cx2 is the squared length of tip minus xpos; cxuv is (puv second, minus puv first) times `isqrt` of cx2 times pn2 (unsigned 64-bit wraparound product); pop the last orientation (fail when there is none) and the prediction is xuv plus cxuv when it is true, xuv minus cxuv otherwise (64-bit wraparound), each component `idiv` pn2 and kept with `w32`. In every other case: when nd is below p the prediction is entry nd, otherwise entry p minus 1 when p is above 0, otherwise zero. `isqrt(n)` treats n as unsigned 64-bit: 0 gives 0; otherwise r starts 1 and a copy of n is divided by 4 while it is at least 2, doubling r each time; then repeat r becomes (r plus n over r) over 2 until r times r is at most n (all unsigned 64-bit, wraparound).

Geometric normal values (pc must be 2): set up the normal transform's q values; for each p from 0 to the length of `d2c` minus 1 with c `d2c[p]`: the center is `posof` of the entry `v2d[vert(t, c)]`; for every corner a in `around(t, c)` add the cross product of (`posof` of the entry of `nxt(a)`'s vertex minus center) and (`posof` of the entry of `prv(a)`'s vertex minus center) to a 64-bit sum (wraparound); s is the sum of the absolute components; when s is above 2 to the 29 each component becomes itself `idiv` (s `idiv` 2 to the 29). Then canonicalize: when s (recomputed) is 0 the vector is (`ce`, 0, 0); else x becomes x times `ce` `idiv` s, y likewise, and z is `ce` minus the absolute x minus the absolute y, negated when z was below 0. When the next flip bit is set negate all three. Then to octahedral: when x is at least 0, s is y plus `ce` and t is z plus `ce`; otherwise s is the absolute z when y is below 0 and `mv` minus the absolute z otherwise, and t is the absolute y when z is below 0 and `mv` minus the absolute y otherwise; then canonical: when (s, t) is (0, 0), (0, `mv`) or (`mv`, 0) both become `mv`; else when s is 0 and t above `ce`, t becomes 2 times `ce` minus t; else when s is `mv` and t below `ce`, t becomes 2 times `ce` minus t; else when t is `mv` and s below `ce`, s becomes 2 times `ce` minus s; else when t is 0 and s above `ce`, s becomes 2 times `ce` minus s. That (s, t) is the prediction for the normal transform.

Transform data and final values (after all of the decoder's portable values, attribute by attribute in order). Generic: nothing to read; the values are the ones read. Integer: nothing to read; each portable value becomes the attribute's data type: low 8 bits for uint8, low 8 signed for int8, low 16 for uint16, low 16 signed for int16, low 32 unsigned for uint32, as is for int32; other data types fail. Quantized: read the minimum per component (`f32v()`), the range (`f32v()`) and the bit count (byte, 1 to 30); delta is the range over (2 to the bits minus 1) in single precision, and each value is the portable value converted to `f32` times delta plus the component's minimum, in single precision. Normal: read the bit count (byte, 2 to 30); with `mv` 2 to the bits minus 2 and scale 2 over `mv` in single precision, each entry (s, t) gives in single precision y as s times scale minus 1 and z as t times scale minus 1, x as 1 minus the absolute y minus the absolute z; o is the larger of minus x and 0; y goes up by o when y is below 0 and down by o otherwise, the same for z; n2 is x squared plus y squared plus z squared (added in that order); when n2 is below 0.000001 the normal is (0, 0, 0), otherwise d is 1 over the square root of n2 and the normal is (x times d, y times d, z times d). Any `bad` read fails. Each attribute's final entry values then go to `values` point by point through its point map, starting at `att_first`.

`decode(src)` takes a read-only byte slice, empties every output and state list, copies src to `buf` and sets `pos` 0. The header is "DRACO", major and minor bytes that must both be 2, an encoder type byte that must be 1 (triangle mesh), a method byte (0 sequential, 1 edgebreaker, else fail) and flags (`u16v()`); metadata is skipped when flags bitwise-and 0x8000 is set. Edgebreaker reads the traversal type byte (0 or 2, else fail), runs `edgebreaker()`, the seams, the slot tables and the points, and then continues reading attributes from the remembered attribute data start; sequential runs `sequential()`. Then `attributes`. It gives true only when every step worked and `bad` is not set; on failure the outputs are emptied and `why` says why.
